WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:02.580
CHRIS KNOWLTON: My name is
Chris Knowlton, as you heard.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.680
I'm with Wowza Media Systems.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.360
And our company got
started, actually,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.559
when our founders,
in 2005, thought they

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:10.850
wanted to start a new business.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:15.010
And they weren't really sure
which-- this is not mine,

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.929
I just want you to
realize that-- they

00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:19.970
wanted to get into video.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.490
They thought it was
emerging, and they thought

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:22.781
it would be really interesting.

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:26.480
So they started a video
blogging business,

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.240
and they found while
they were trying

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.630
to build out the business that
they couldn't find a media

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.471
server on the market
that met their needs.

00:00:33.471 --> 00:00:35.595
They couldn't find one that
was scalable, reliable,

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:37.790
and flexible enough.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.630
And so as part of the business,
they developed their own media

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:41.157
server.

00:00:41.157 --> 00:00:42.740
And then they went
out to trade shows,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.280
and people kept coming to
them, and saying, yeah,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.863
I don't care about
the video blogging,

00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:48.466
but what server are you
using because I want that?

00:00:48.466 --> 00:00:49.840
And so they did
a pivot, and they

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.464
started a new business
around something

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:54.020
called Wowza Media Server.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.520
And so it's been on the market
since-- it actually went out

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.200
in pre-production in 2006.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.320
By the time it launched
in February of 2007,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.630
it had 1,000 users
on it already.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:06.760
We now have folks in over
150 countries using it,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.100
and it's arguably now the most
widely used streaming media

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.050
server in the world.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.046
Oh, there we go.

00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:18.835
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:18.835 --> 00:01:19.335
That's good.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.004
It's used for a lot of things.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:29.420
One of the nice
things about it is

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.450
that it's been in the market, as
I mentioned, since early 2007.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.090
Every year it has won multiple
awards, both reader's choice

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.180
awards and editor's
choice awards

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.690
for innovation and for support.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.565
And one of the nice
things about it, I guess,

00:01:40.565 --> 00:01:43.290
is when it came to market
what it was really competing

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.300
with a lot was Flash Media
Server, Windows Media

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.350
Server, then IIS Media
Services, and RealNetworks.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.440
And eventually, all of
those have pretty much

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.950
either gone away or
changed their focus.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.870
Microsoft's no longer shipping
Media Server software,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.050
RealNetworks is not,
and Adobe's focused

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.970
on their OTT, or their
TV everywhere offering

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.130
through Primetime.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.420
And so we came to the
market with something

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.620
that supported
multiple protocols,

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.110
multiple ways of
streaming, so not just

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.320
Windows Media or just IIS
Media, or real streaming.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.780
But a combination
of all of those.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.910
Basically, you could
take a single workflow

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.900
with Wowza Media
Server, and replace

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.990
multiple parallel workflows
that you had with all

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.101
these other technologies.

00:02:28.101 --> 00:02:29.600
And some of those
other technologies

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.020
were obviously based on using
a certain operating system.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.850
Another thing that
Wowza has done

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:41.900
is based its ability to run not
on any single operating system,

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.262
but on multiple, so it'll run
on almost any machine you have.

00:02:45.262 --> 00:02:46.470
So it makes it very flexible.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.170
It's made it very attractive
to a lot of folks.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.600
It was originally aimed at
developers and folks who were

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.030
streaming media professionals.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.840
And over time, what
we've tried to do

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.600
is to continue to simplify
streaming, not only by offering

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.080
multiple formats in
a single product,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.520
and the ability to ingest
from almost any encoder,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.520
and deliver to almost
any screen on the planet,

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.950
but to do it in a simpler way.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.600
And to make it
easier and easier,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.950
lowering the bar to get
involved in streaming,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.890
and not having to be so
technically literate to get

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:15.390
started.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.730
So let's see.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.450
What did I miss?

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:21.950
Anything?

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.580
We're growing fast,
lots of awards.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.580
OK, good.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.680
Move on.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.210
For those of you who might have
heard of Wowza Media Server

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.709
originally, it's now actually
called Wowza Streaming Engine.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:35.562
We changed the name last
year, and we released a user

00:03:35.562 --> 00:03:36.270
interface for it.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.910
Before then it was all about
XML and command line, which

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.332
is great for a lot of you.

00:03:40.332 --> 00:03:42.540
For a lot of folks, it was
a little bit intimidating.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.280
So we put a nice wrapper
around it, web-based UI.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.640
So that even from
your Android phone,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.380
your tablet, your desktop,
wherever you are you

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.700
can control your server just
by going into a web browser

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.140
to a URL.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:57.790
Let's see.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.880
Did I miss anything?

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.500
I did mention the
multiple formats,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.020
the idea that you can stream
in multiple formats not

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:04.954
just a single one.

00:04:04.954 --> 00:04:06.120
You can do smooth streaming.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.880
You can do HTTP
Dynamic Streaming,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.920
Adobe's adaptive
streaming format,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.360
or as well as our TMPN and RTSP.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.070
How many here
actually know what I'm

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.952
talking about when I
mention these acronyms?

00:04:16.952 --> 00:04:17.660
OK, a few of you.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.829
Excellent.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:20.130
Let me just put it this way.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.730
No matter what device you
have, an iPhone, an Android,

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:27.860
a Windows Phone, a television,
a set-top box, Wowza Streaming

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.000
Engine can put out
a stream in a format

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.740
that those devices will
be able to playback from.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.600
So you no longer have to have
multiple sets of technologies

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.010
to hit all those endpoints.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.820
So who uses Wowza is a
question I get asked.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.460
Usually the question I ask
back is, who doesn't use Wowza?

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.369
And what I mean by that is that
there are almost no streaming

00:04:49.369 --> 00:04:51.910
scenarios that you can think of
where Wowza is not being used

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.940
today, from over the top
scenarios that you see over

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:01.970
in the lower right there to live
events to inside enterprises

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:04.560
and universities.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.716
For surveillance capabilities
as well as planes,

00:05:07.716 --> 00:05:09.840
trains, automobiles, and
cruise ships for folks who

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.690
are putting in their own
on-demand delivery service.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.580
And increasingly not
just because they're

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.630
going to a fixed television
mounted to a wall,

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:21.140
but because folks in
the hospitality business

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.860
are finding that if somebody
brings their favorite tablet

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.440
with them, they want to
go around the cruise ship

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.481
and watch their content
no matter where they are.

00:05:27.481 --> 00:05:29.150
And Wowza can stream
to all of them,

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.620
and give folks that great
experience no matter where they

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.050
are in that cruise
ship, as it were.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.901
It's also used for-- we don't
really address it here--

00:05:39.901 --> 00:05:41.983
but it's also used for
things like real time video

00:05:41.983 --> 00:05:42.560
conferencing.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.380
So a lot of folks have
built online video platform

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.560
around that capability to
deliver content and have

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:52.570
video chat and conversations
using Wowza Streaming Engine.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:57.770
So here is the marketecture
slide for how Wowza works.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:02.236
In one side you see, in
the upper left, the input

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:02.860
and publishing.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.620
And what it's showing is that
we can take an input from almost

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.780
any source, whether it be
a camera that can put out

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:12.560
an IP stream, an IP camera
or a network camera,

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.810
whether it be content that
you're pulling from a drive

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.810
somewhere, say Google Storage,
whether it's internet radio,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.560
or even just you can
pick up an iPhone

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.810
and run a free app that we'll
provide you called Wowza

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.390
GoCoder on it, and
you can hold it up,

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:27.848
and point it at something that's
happening that's exciting,

00:06:27.848 --> 00:06:29.820
and you can stream
it out to the world.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.960
And we can do that
from almost any encoder

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:36.050
just because we support so
many incoming protocols.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.870
So once it gets to
Wowza Streaming Engine,

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.440
which is just a
piece of software

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.280
that runs either on
premises or in the cloud,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.420
and what we really want to
talk today about is the cloud,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.000
and how it runs very well
in Google Compute Engine.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.689
Once we get it, we can take
that one stream coming in.

00:06:53.689 --> 00:06:55.730
And typically what we
recommend is people send us

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:59.230
one HD String if it's a live
event, and we'll take it,

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.200
and we can transcode
it and transrate it,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.380
which means we can
take in one HD stream,

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.929
and then make different
incremental streams that

00:07:05.929 --> 00:07:07.970
can be used for what's
called adaptive streaming.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:09.760
So if you've ever
watched Netflix or Hulu

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.344
and noticed that every once in
awhile it'll get a little fuzzy

00:07:12.344 --> 00:07:14.176
and then it'll snap
back and be sharp again,

00:07:14.176 --> 00:07:15.790
but you never see
the word buffering.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.410
That's because a few years ago
adaptive streaming came out

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:23.110
onto the market-- about 2008--
and it allows your player

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:25.700
to automatically adjust
as your bandwidth adjusts

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.110
or as what you're doing
on your device changes.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.001
Maybe you've got more
load going and your CPU

00:07:32.001 --> 00:07:33.500
is getting bogged
down a little bit.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.025
It'll adjust to a lower stream,
one that's easier for it

00:07:36.025 --> 00:07:38.074
to playback or to pull
across the network.

00:07:38.074 --> 00:07:39.490
And it will allow
you to play back

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:41.207
with a seamless
experience, or maybe

00:07:41.207 --> 00:07:42.790
you just get a little
bit of fuzziness

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.748
for a couple of seconds,
but it keeps on going.

00:07:44.748 --> 00:07:47.720
You don't have to wait
for a buffering symbol.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.356
And so that's what adaptive
bitrate streaming is all about.

00:07:50.356 --> 00:07:51.730
And Wowza Streaming
Engine allows

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.560
you to bring in a
single stream and create

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.131
these multiple streams needed
for adaptive bitrate streaming.

00:07:56.131 --> 00:07:57.630
And that's its
probably most popular

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.960
use today, although
it's still really used

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:03.610
a lot for both RTMP,
traditionally flash streaming,

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.500
and RTSP streaming, which
is an even older version

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.490
of streaming, a traditional
stateful streaming protocol

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.570
used by many legacy devices,
especially in emerging markets

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.490
now.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.240
And then when it goes
out, as I mentioned,

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.582
it can go out in many
different streaming formats,

00:08:18.582 --> 00:08:20.040
and it can go to
almost any player.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.550
You can pick a player.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.440
You can send it to a
built-in player on an iPhone

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:25.840
or an Android device.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.480
You can pick a player like
JW Player or Flowplayer

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.620
that plays back almost across
any device and any screen,

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.260
and makes it even simpler
because they help you customize

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.020
the player and everything
so that you have this really

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.470
great experience
that's customized

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:42.872
for your content and your
delivery for your end users.

00:08:42.872 --> 00:08:44.580
Other things that we
can do, just in case

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.080
it's interesting
to you, we can also

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.580
do things like closed
captioning, a key requirement

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.135
for the FCC these days.

00:08:52.135 --> 00:08:54.862
Lots of API extensibility
here, so anything

00:08:54.862 --> 00:08:57.320
that you can do through the UI
or through the command line,

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.702
you can also do directly
through the API.

00:08:59.702 --> 00:09:01.660
So you can actually build
this into an offering

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.540
that you have completely
transparent to your end user

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.910
that you're using this product,
and deliver the content

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.732
to them.

00:09:08.732 --> 00:09:10.440
And then we have a
couple of other things

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.235
that used to be called
premium add-ons.

00:09:12.235 --> 00:09:13.610
Now they're just
part of the base

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:17.431
price of the product which is
$65 a month for the software.

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:18.930
I mentioned the
transcoding earlier,

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.270
that capability to
bring in the content.

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:24.420
Not only split it up
into different bitrates,

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.510
but also bring in content that
maybe was encoded on an older

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.520
encoder, change the codec,
the way it's compressed,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.370
and then spit it out
as multiple versions

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.180
and in multiple formats.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:38.190
We also have DRM capabilities,
so obviously for a lot of you

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.810
if you're delivering
studio content,

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:42.810
you need to have that content
protected all the way across.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.440
So Wowza Streaming Engine can
take in protected content,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.630
unprotect it, decrypt
it, and then send it

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.140
out to all those
different devices

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:53.810
in multiple formats, encrypted,
and protected in multiple ways.

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.500
And we have built-in integration
with three DRM service

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:57.610
providers.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:01.160
And it also works across in
some very wide DRM platforms

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.280
if you want to do a
little work on your own.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.440
And then the last thing
is network DVR, end DVR,

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.250
that capability to
pause a live stream

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:11.616
or jump ahead or jump back
in the stream that you get

00:10:11.616 --> 00:10:13.574
and have been able to
get for a number of years

00:10:13.574 --> 00:10:15.170
with set-top boxes and TiVo.

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:19.690
All right.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:21.580
So a typical workflow,
to take what I just

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:24.030
showed you there is, for
a live event anyways,

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:25.709
you have some kind of camera.

00:10:25.709 --> 00:10:26.750
You have some live event.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.590
Maybe you have multiple
cameras, like we have today.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.048
There are actually
multiple cameras

00:10:30.048 --> 00:10:31.810
in the room today
capturing this event.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:33.840
And if we were streaming
live-- and next time

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.317
I hope we're streaming live
with Wowza and Google--

00:10:36.317 --> 00:10:38.400
you would actually pull
in those multiple streams.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.940
And you can mix them in
real time in something

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.550
like-- you can either just
pull it into a single encoder

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:49.040
or you can bring it into an
encoding product such as tell

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.406
a Telestream Wirecast or
NewTek TriCaster, if you've

00:10:51.406 --> 00:10:52.470
heard of those.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:56.140
Bring in multiple streams and
do live mixing of those streams,

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:59.690
and switching shots, and
putting on overlays and titles.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.850
So that's probably what
you would do at a venue.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.290
So a lot of folks use
Wowza Streaming Engine

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.550
when they're at a
live event, a football

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:10.020
game, a house of worship
service, a live concert,

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.070
an event like this even.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:12.820
And they capture this
event, and then they

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.360
send out one single
stream that's

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:19.460
been compressed with the H.264
video codec and AC audio codec.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:23.250
And they'll send that to a Wowza
Streaming Engine in the cloud.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.070
And there we'll do
all that transrating,

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:28.270
the creating of the multiple
bitrates files or streams,

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.650
and we'll repackage it
into those multiple formats

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.820
that I talked about-- smooth,
streaming, HLS, HDS-- and then

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:34.820
we'll publish it.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.830
And you can actually publish
directly from Google Compute

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:41.090
Engine instances right
to a bunch of end users

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.140
who are on their phones, whether
they happen to be streaming

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:47.270
in emerging markets over these
traditional streaming protocols

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.450
that I mentioned--
RTMP and RTSP--

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:53.490
or you can publish directly
out to a CDN such as Fastly,

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.070
which does a great
job of scaling

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.506
out all HTTP type streaming.

00:11:57.506 --> 00:11:59.880
So you get that great adaptive
bitrate streaming plus you

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:00.838
get global scalability.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.310
And then you hit any
screen that's out there.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.060
Because there are
enough players out there

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.030
that will allow you to deliver
to any screen in the world just

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.680
using one or two formats
such as Apple HLS.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.910
So why do we use Google?

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.370
We actually started
using Google--

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:21.470
I'm going to jump ahead--
we started using Google

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:25.530
two years ago because we saw
some things in Google Cloud

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.780
Platform that we were not seeing
with some other cloud providers

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.950
that we'd been testing on.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:34.015
The first thing is that its
startup speed was much faster.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:40.110
To some extent, this is a
lot of anecdotal as opposed

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.460
to performance testing.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.796
But our engineers realized
that the startup speed

00:12:43.796 --> 00:12:45.420
was about twice as
fast as what we were

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.380
getting on other platforms.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.070
From a storage
performance perspective,

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:50.620
the I/O was much better.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:55.770
We were able to move data in
and out of drives and on disk

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.730
and back very quickly, which
can be really critical if you're

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:02.517
pulling in a live stream and
recording it in real time,

00:13:02.517 --> 00:13:04.100
and then delivering
it out, especially

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.920
with that network
DVR capability.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.710
And then you want
to pull it out later

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.300
so you can have
this archive file

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:12.480
that you play back on demand.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.810
We also found that with
some cloud platforms

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:18.100
we've been using, customers were
finding that their experience

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:19.590
was inconsistent.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.720
So they would be running our
server on a cloud platform,

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.070
and they would find
that it would not always

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:27.150
be a consistent behavior.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.941
And sometimes they
might be able to support

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:32.230
500 concurrent connections,
and other times it was 1,000.

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.570
And it seemed to be a
bandwidth limitation where

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:37.520
perhaps too many people were
sharing the same network pipe.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:41.322
We don't see those problems
at all with Google Cloud.

00:13:41.322 --> 00:13:42.780
And then from a
pricing perspective

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.540
it was attractive
not only for us,

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:47.460
but for our customers who are
just running Wowza Streaming

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.739
Engine on Google, because
they could not only, as it was

00:13:51.739 --> 00:13:54.030
mentioned earlier, not only
do you get to sustained use

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:57.359
pricing where the longer you use
it the more the pricing drops,

00:13:57.359 --> 00:13:59.650
but also for those folks who
are running the short term

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.870
events or the bursty
traffic, and they wanted

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:03.690
to be able to just
pay for what they use,

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:07.952
the 23 minutes,
not the full hour.

00:14:07.952 --> 00:14:09.410
And the final thing
that we like is

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:10.826
that when we're
creating an image,

00:14:10.826 --> 00:14:13.390
we don't have to create
an image for each region,

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:16.110
as you might have to do
on some other platforms.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:16.860
Made it very easy.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:20.000
We create a single image,
we populate it out there,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.820
put it in a storage bucket,
and everyone can get to it.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.120
We don't have to create
multiple versions,

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:27.415
just specific for a region.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.280
So what's coming next?

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:36.649
I'm showing here on
one side the steps

00:14:36.649 --> 00:14:38.690
that it takes to get
started with Wowza Streaming

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:40.200
Engine on Google Compute.

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.740
It's not very difficult.
There are a number of steps

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.365
that you have to go
through, but if you

00:14:47.365 --> 00:14:49.090
want to do this on
a regular basis,

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:52.960
this might get a
little bit tedious

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:55.880
if you had to do it all the time
and with multiple instances.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:59.030
So there's certainly a
much easier way to do it,

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:01.630
and that's using
Click-to-Deploy.

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.240
Soon I'm hoping that
we're going to be

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.760
able to offer the first
commercial Click-to-Deploy

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:06.380
offering.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.701
Today, if you go to
the Google Cloud site,

00:15:08.701 --> 00:15:10.200
you will find that
there are a bunch

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:13.240
of Click-to-Deploy offerings
out there all open source where

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:18.490
you can go and very quickly
click on an offering,

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:21.330
make a couple of configuration
settings, and then launch it.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.460
And it just saves a whole lot
of steps in configuration.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:26.360
And we're going to
be offering that.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.040
You'll also be able,
very soon, to start

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:30.354
doing things where you take
a whole bunch of resources

00:15:30.354 --> 00:15:31.770
in a similar way,
you could say, I

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.800
want to fire up three instances.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:35.350
I want to use this storage.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:36.530
I want to push it to Fastly.

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.180
And you can do it all through,
essentially, one configuration

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:39.460
script.

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:41.890
You'll be able to do that very
soon too, so that once you have

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:43.250
your settings the
way you want them

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.340
you can just fire it off
the same way every time.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.430
Speaking of Fastly,
we're also going

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.400
to be offering Fastly
integration soon.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:53.779
So today, you can do it.

00:15:53.779 --> 00:15:55.070
There are a few steps involved.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:56.570
We're going to make
it a lot easier.

00:15:58.764 --> 00:16:00.430
There are two ways
you can do it really.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.221
The first way is if
you have a live stream,

00:16:02.221 --> 00:16:04.360
you can push it to
a storage bucket.

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:05.609
Makes it very easy.

00:16:05.609 --> 00:16:07.400
And then Fastly just
picks it up from there

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.232
and distributes globally.

00:16:10.232 --> 00:16:11.940
We also have very low
latency connections

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:15.750
because Fastly is co-located
or has the interconnects

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:19.980
where Google Cloud
platform is located.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.770
So the connections
there are really

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:24.590
low latency, which is
very good for us as far

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.610
as getting content
to your users faster.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:30.260
So the TV, they don't hear the
cheer on the TV several seconds

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.840
before they see it
on their device.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.340
And then the last
thing is if you're

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.090
doing on-demand streaming,
you're pulling the content off

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.140
of a Google Compute
Engine instance

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.870
using Fastly as your
distribution mechanism.

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:45.270
As Simon mentioned earlier,
they have the origin shield

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.144
capability, which
protects the origin

00:16:47.144 --> 00:16:49.560
from getting too many requests
at once from all those edge

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:50.699
nodes that Fastly runs.

00:16:50.699 --> 00:16:52.990
They just have a single
request come back all the time,

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:56.050
and then feed all those nodes.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:57.649
And so that workflow
that I showed

00:16:57.649 --> 00:16:59.190
you earlier would
look like this when

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:00.398
you put Fastly in the middle.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:04.667
So that's it.

00:17:04.667 --> 00:17:06.500
If you have questions,
we'll have Q&amp;A later.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:07.720
I'll be happy to answer then.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:09.109
And of course, I'll
stick around also

00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:10.567
as well if you have
other questions

00:17:10.567 --> 00:17:12.129
or feel free to email.

